  "notes": "{散|ち}る describes things scattering or falling apart, especially petals and leaves.\n\nTRANSITIVITY:\n- Type: {自動詞|じどうし} (intransitive)\n- Pair: {散|ち}らす (transitive, to scatter something)\n\nASPECT (ている):\n{散|ち}っている indicates a state where petals/leaves have fallen and are scattered.\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- {桜|さくら}が{散|ち}る: cherry blossoms fall\n- {花|はな}びらが{散|ち}る: petals scatter\n- {落|お}ち{葉|ば}が{散|ち}る: fallen leaves scatter\n- {人|ひと}が{散|ち}る: people disperse\n\nCULTURE:\n{桜|さくら}が{散|ち}る (cherry blossoms falling) is a significant image in Japanese aesthetics, symbolizing the transient beauty of life ({無常|むじょう}). The phrase {散|ち}り{際|ぎわ} (moment of falling) describes a graceful end.\n\nRELATED:\n- {散歩|さんぽ}: a walk (different kanji compound)\n- {散|ち}り{散|ぢ}り: scattered about",
